  "question": {
    "text": "If $0 < x < \\pi$ and $\\cos x+\\sin x=\\frac{1}{2}$, then $\\tan x$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "rEaNc",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{(1-\\sqrt{7})}{4}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "JMPIk",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{(4-\\sqrt{7})}{3}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "M2B4u",
      "text": "\n$-\\frac{(4+\\sqrt{7})}{3}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "XNXWf",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{(1+\\sqrt{7})}{4}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\cos x+\\sin x=\\frac{1}{2} \\Rightarrow 1+\\sin 2 x=\\frac{1}{4} \\Rightarrow \\sin 2 x=-\\frac{3}{4}$, so $x$ is obtuse and $\\frac{2 \\tan x}{1+\\tan ^2 x}=-\\frac{3}{4} \\Rightarrow 3 \\tan ^2 x+8 \\tan x+3=0$\n$\\therefore \\tan x=\\frac{-8 \\pm \\sqrt{64-36}}{6}=\\frac{-4 \\pm \\sqrt{7}}{3}$\n$\\because \\tan x < 0 \\quad \\therefore \\tan x=\\frac{-4-\\sqrt{7}}{3}$",
    "image": null
  },
